PROJECT GOAL: We aim to verify if the user needs to set up a build environment to resolve the issue:

1. Does not need build environment
   (The solutions/answers can be validated without build environment, e.g., it's a conceptual issue, documentation issue, or can be verified with simple code analysis)

2. Can be dockerized without any issue
   (The solutions must require a build environment to be verified)

3. Requires build environment but hard to be dockerized or cannot be reproduced now
   (The solution must require a build environment, but it is difficult to containerize for reasons such as: hardware dependencies, network dependencies, external API access with authentication, timing-dependent behaviors, requiring MacOS, multiple users interacting simultaneously, licensed software, etc.)

IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
- Choose category 1 whenever possible. If the LLM can analyze the issue and verify the solution without needing to build or run the code, always select category 1.
- Only classify an issue as requiring a build environment (categories 2 or 3) when it's ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to actually build and run the code to verify if the solution works.

GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2022-02-11T19:20:45Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Reducing memory usage
Description: ### What are you trying to achieve?
I'm processing batches of 200-400MB TIFF files on an instance with 1GB of memory. It is occasionally not enough. I'm trying to understand what I can do better to manage memory.

### Have you searched for similar questions?
Yes, I've landed on #138 and related issues regarding streaming. But I'm not sure if this is the best approach.

My use case requires me to clone and downsample the entire image to be analyzed. I then use the analysis results for processing the full-size image. If I am to change the file to a stream, I may not be able to split it into two parts (one, downsized, for analysis and one for processing).

I currently start the instance on `node:14-alpine` container with `--optimize_for_size --max_old_space_size=320 --gc_interval=50`. Other than streaming, I'm also considering adding an artificial delay between jobs to ensure that the memory has time to empty out.

I would really appreciate some guidance here. Thank you so much!

### Are you able to provide a minimal, standalone code sample that demonstrates this question?
```javascript

// create a reduced-size image sample
const data = await sharp(image).clone().resize(500).toBuffer();

// this object will store values that will be used for full-size image manipulation
const prescriptions = {};

// determine values for full-size image manipulation
const channelNames = ["red", "green", "blue"];
const prescriptionsCalculators = channelNames.map(async (channel) => {
  const forAnalysis = await sharp(data)
  .extractChannel(channel)
  .toColorspace("b-w")
  .blur(1)
  .toBuffer();

  const stats = await sharp(forAnalysis).stats();
  const { channels } = stats;

  const range = [channels[0].min, channels[0].max];
  const linear = levels(range[0], range[1]);

  prescriptions[channel] = { linear, range };
  return await linear;
});
await Promise.all(prescriptionsCalculators);

// use the above data to process full-size image
const channels = {};
const channelEffects = channelNames.map(async (channel) => {
  const result = sharp(reversed)
    .extractChannel(channel)
    .toColorspace("b-w")
    .linear(...prescriptions[channel].linear)
    .toBuffer();
  channels[channel] = result;
  return result;
});
await Promise.all(channelEffects);

// reassemble channels into RGB
const positive = sharp(await channels["red"]).joinChannel([
  await channels["green"],
  await channels["blue"],
]);

return positive;
```


